import chromadb

client = chromadb.PersistentClient(
    path="./secure_vector_db"
)

collection = client.get_or_create_collection(
    name="company_documents"
)

def store_document(
    document,
    authenticated_tenant_id,
    document_id
):
    # Tenant identity comes from trusted
    # backend authentication, not user input.

    collection.add(
        ids=[document_id],
        documents=[document],
        metadatas=[{
            "tenant_id": authenticated_tenant_id
        }]
    )

def search_documents(query, tenant_id):
    return collection.query(
        query_texts=[query],
        n_results=5,
        where={
            "tenant_id": tenant_id
        }
    )
```

This demonstrates tenant filtering, not complete database security. ChromaDB's filtering does not itself authenticate users or provide a full multi-tenant authorization boundary. Production applications must protect database connectivity, validate tenant identity in the backend, restrict database permissions and test isolation.

